      "response": "{\n  \"design\": \"Regression Discontinuity Design (RDD)\",\n  \"instrument\": \"The score cutoff at 70, which creates a sharp jump in participation probability, serving as an exogenous source of treatment variation near the threshold.\",\n  \"estimand\": \"The local average treatment effect (LATE) at the cutoff score of 70, i.e., the causal effect of job-training participation on employment for individuals whose participation status is changed by the cutoff.\",\n  \"global_generalization\": \"No, it does not automatically generalize to everyone. The effect is identified only for the subpopulation near the cutoff (compliers at the threshold), and extrapolation to other scores or the entire population requires additional assumptions.\",\n  \"assumptions\": [\n    \"The score (running variable) cannot be precisely manipulated by individuals or administrators around the cutoff.\",\n    \"Potential outcomes (employment) vary smoothly through the cutoff in the absence of treatment (continuity of potential outcome functions).\",\n    \"No other policy changes or interventions occur at the score of 70 that could affect employment.\",\n    \"The probability of participation jumps discontinuously at the cutoff (first stage is strong).\",\n    \"Individuals with scores just above and below the cutoff are comparable in terms of potential outcomes (local randomization assumption).\",\n    \"The treatment effect is constant or the local average treatment effect is well-defined for compliers at the cutoff (monotonicity or no defiers).\"\n  ]\n}",
