      "response": "**Recommendation: Reject automatic restrictive supervision based solely on the ML risk score.** Calibration within demographic groups means the predicted probability matches observed recidivism rates for each group *in the historical data*. That justifies using the score as a *screening* tool to prioritize human review, but it does **not** justify automatic deprivation of liberty. Calibration says nothing about whether the prediction is causal, whether the label (arrest) is unbiased, or whether the score\u2019s accuracy is stable under new conditions.\n\n**Key analytical points:**\n\n- **Base rates & asymmetric errors:** Severe offenses are rare (e.g., 2% base rate). Even a well-calibrated model with 90% sensitivity and 95% specificity yields a positive predictive value of ~27%. Thus, ~73% of those flagged as \u201chigh risk\u201d would *not* commit the offense. Automatic restrictive supervision would wrongly detain many innocent people. The cost of a false positive (loss of liberty) is far graver than a false negative (missed offense), so the threshold must be set with explicit, democratically debated error weights\u2014not a fixed statistical cutoff.\n- **Causation vs. prediction:** The model predicts risk from historical correlations (e.g., prior arrests, neighborhood). It does not identify *why* someone offends. Restrictive supervision changes the environment (e.g., curfews, monitoring), which may alter the very causal pathways\u2014possibly reducing risk (good) or increasing it (e.g., job loss from restrictions, leading to more crime). You cannot assume the prediction remains valid under the intervention.\n- **Selective labels & feedback loops:** Arrests are not ground truth. Police deployment changes who is observed; if the system flags certain groups, increased surveillance will generate more arrests in those groups, reinforcing the model\u2019s predictions\u2014a feedback loop that inflates apparent risk without proving actual dangerousness.\n- **Rights & due process:** Automatic restriction bypasses individualized adjudication. The defendant has no chance to contest the data, present mitigating evidence, or challenge the model\u2019s logic. This violates basic procedural fairness.\n- **Distribution shift & strategic adaptation:** Economic downturns, policy changes, or defendants learning the features (e.g., avoiding arrests) will shift the risk distribution. The model\u2019s calibration will decay, but the policy would continue to apply a fixed threshold.\n\n**Concrete deployment/evaluation design (if any use is considered):**\n\n1. **Use only as a triage tool:** The score triggers a mandatory, time-limited (e.g., 48-hour) human review by a judge or trained officer, who must document independent reasons for restriction. No automatic action.\n2. **Prospective evaluation with a holdout group:** Randomly assign flagged defendants to either standard process or the ML-assisted review. Compare outcomes (actual violent offenses, not arrests) over 12 months, adjusting for differential surveillance.\n3. **Transparent thresholds:** Publish the score\u2019s sensitivity, specificity, and PPV at the chosen threshold. Require a minimum PPV of 50% (i.e., majority of flagged are true positives) before any restriction is allowed.\n4. **Stopping rules:** Pause the system if (a) calibration drift exceeds 5 percentage points in any demographic group, (b) the false-positive rate among flagged rises above 70%, or (c) any audit finds disparate impact on protected classes not justified by actual offense rates.\n5. **Sunset clause:** Reauthorize every 6 months only after independent audit of outcomes, including a randomized trial of the intervention\u2019s effect on recidivism and collateral harms (employment, housing).\n\n**Conclusion:** The evidence supports cautious, supervised use as a decision aid\u2014never as an automated sentence. Without these safeguards, the policy is unjustified.",
      "grade_details": {
